                <pb facs="0517" n="397" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg397"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 Steering Committee/161: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany (Gordon) to the Secretary of
                            State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-11-18T15:00:00+01:00">November 18,
                            1932—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 18—12:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>221. The French disarmament plan has encountered general antagonism in Germany.
                    It has been criticized semi-officially as not being a disarmament plan but
                    merely a political organization of Europe to safeguard French security by
                    maintaining the <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi>, and as not realizing
                    Germany’s equality claim. The only idea found sound was that of standardizing
                    continental armies. Entire German press <pb facs="0518" n="398" xml:id="pg398"/>
                    destructively critical ever since publication of text which right press
                    described as “grotesque”, “indiscussable” and “unacceptable” while left press
                    voiced keen disappointment and complained of plan’s ambiguousness.</p>
                <p>On the other hand initial reaction to Simon’s Geneva speech has been favorable in moderate and social
                    democratic press which considers it a plea for German equality. Right press
                    regards it as intended to soothe Germany and to bring her back to Geneva but as
                    according her only “moral” equality.</p>
                <p>Copy by mail to Geneva.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gordon</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
